WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.500
Boris Johnson counting his final days as British
prime minister - in disgrace, but still in

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.630
office till his replacement’s chosen.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:13.780
Why did the Conservative Party accept all
the lying and the U-turns for so long?

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:18.330
My guest this week in London is Sir Malcolm
Rifkind, veteran conservative politician who’s

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.390
served as both defense and foreign secretary.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.720
No fan of Boris the politician.

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.140
“I never thought he should have been prime
minister in the first place.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.859
He was the wrong person here.”

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:32.980
So what happens now to all the former ministers
and officials who indulged Johnson’s boosterism

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.190
and falsehoods?

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:36.960
Will the Tory party clean them out?

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.100
How much did Conservatives really do to help
Ukraine prepare for war?

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:46.610
And when all’s said and done, will the next
British prime minister just be more of the

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:47.610
same?

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.020
Key questions this week on Conflict Zone.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:59.440
Sir Malcolm Rifkind, welcome to Conflict Zone.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:00.640
Thank you.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.040
You wrote the other day that Boris Johnson
trashed or tried to trash many of the conventions

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.030
that protect British liberties and the country's
unwritten constitution.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:12.540
That's a very serious charge.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.439
If it's true, why do you think it took your
party so long to get rid of him?

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:24.840
Well, I think, first of all, I'm not suggesting
he thought of it as trashing all the conventions.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.270
The problem with Boris Johnson is he doesn't
think through the implications of what he

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:32.070
does and sometimes the seriousness of what
he does.

00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.010
I don't think he's immoral, I think he's amoral.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.149
You're letting him off the hook here.

00:01:35.149 --> 00:01:36.430
I'm not letting him off the hook.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:41.829
I'm simply saying he acted in an incredibly
reckless way because Britain, unlike virtually

00:01:41.829 --> 00:01:44.719
every other country in the world, does not
have a written constitution.

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:49.329
For our system to work it relies on what you've
just described as the conventions, whereby

00:01:49.329 --> 00:01:56.500
Parliament and ministers accept their responsibilities
and desist from certain kinds of activity.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:01.370
And he seemed to believe that in his own case,
because he had such a large majority in Parliament,

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:04.149
he could get away with that and he found it
couldn't.

00:02:04.149 --> 00:02:07.619
And the party let him get away with it because
the writing had been on the wall for some

00:02:07.619 --> 00:02:08.619
time, hadn't it?

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:13.660
Two ethics advisers resigning, one saying
the prime minister put him in an odious position.

00:02:13.660 --> 00:02:18.150
The resignation in June of John Penrose, the
government's own anti-corruption tsar, saying

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:23.050
Johnson had committed a fundamental breach
of the ministerial code over ‘partygate’.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:27.660
At all these junctures, the Conservative Party
looked away.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:28.660
Why?

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:34.020
Okay, let me- I'm going to answer your question,
but I have to proceed by saying that in my

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.800
own case, I never thought he should have been
prime minister in the first place.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.970
He was the wrong person here.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:39.860
I'd love to have him at a dinner party.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:43.840
I did not think his remarkable qualities were
suited to being prime minister of any country,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:47.780
and certainly not United Kingdom, and so did
a large minority of conservatives.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:55.760
But to come to your question, one thing you
didn't mention was- it is a very short time

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:02.330
since he led the Conservative Party to a huge
majority, far greater than anyone expected.

00:03:02.330 --> 00:03:04.120
It’s two and a half years.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:05.120
Yes, precisely.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.240
But when you when you have such a mandate
and no one -even I - suggest we would have

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.090
had that large majority without his leadership
at that time.

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.410
He caught the public mood.

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:19.290
He won large numbers of seats in what are
called the red wall area, the northern parts

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:25.290
of England, which have voted Labour for 70
years and suddenly significant numbers voted

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:26.290
Conservative.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:27.340
And Conservative MPs were returned.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:31.840
So just because he was a vote winner, you
could look away from all the transgressions

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.790
and all the trashing of rights and all that.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:39.130
All I'm saying is it was inevitably going
to be a gradual disillusionment on the part

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:43.420
of those who had thought he was a great guy
and would make a great prime minister.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:49.370
But there was a consciousness that removing
a prime minister from office, not at a general

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:54.810
election, but by his own party - the last
time that happened was Theresa May and the

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.000
time before that was Margaret Thatcher.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.840
So the Conservative Party does have a tradition,
unlike the Labour Party or other parties in

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:09.140
Britain, of if it comes to a judgment that
a prime minister has become a permanent liability

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:13.860
rather than an asset, it can get rid of that
leader very ruthlessly, as it did on this

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:14.860
occasion.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.500
Well, you say very ruthlessly, I would say-

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.739
Well it wasn't very difficult-

00:04:17.739 --> 00:04:19.230
They took their time over this.

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:23.140
Perhaps the most damning comment came last
November from Lord Evans, chairman of the

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:27.580
Committee on Standards in Public Life, about
Johnson's attempt to change the disciplinary

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:31.880
procedure for MPs while one of his own was
under investigation.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.490
Evans called it a very damaging moment for
Parliament and for public standards, and he

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:38.720
gave this unprecedented warning.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:44.260
He said there was a danger that Britain could
slip into being a corrupt country.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.760
Again, the Conservative Party looked away.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.760
Seriously the Tories blind eye has been staggering,
hasn’t it?

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:56.870
You're perfectly entitled to put it in those
terms, but you're using nice, colorful theatrical

00:04:56.870 --> 00:05:03.570
language to mask the fact that in a democracy,
an elected prime minister who has had the

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:08.990
confidence not just of Parliament as a whole,
but of his party, it is quite an awkward thing,

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:14.940
to put it mildly, to suddenly dump him in
the in the middle of a parliament.

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:19.250
And that was a gradual process because his
colleagues in Cabinet and it was only the

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:26.320
Cabinet ultimately who had that power to remove
him, obviously were reluctant to act in such

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.810
a unilateral way, and it would have been surprising
if they weren't.

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:33.620
So I'm not saying I wouldn't have preferred
it if they'd done what they eventually did

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:35.770
six months earlier or a year earlier.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:39.520
I personally would have preferred that, but
then I've already told you I would have preferred

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.180
him never having been in Downing Street in
the first place.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.670
Isn't the real problem with the Conservative
Party that it has for too long put its own

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:52.200
electoral fortunes above the interests of
the British people?

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:57.730
It allowed the prime minister to continue
doing harm and lying, engaging in what the

00:05:57.730 --> 00:06:02.240
eminent historian Peter Hennessy called a
bonfire of the decencies.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.990
Shouldn't the party be hanging its head in
shame that it did this?

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:09.710
Again you're using marvelous, colorful language
and if I was sitting where you’re sitting-

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:10.710
Well Peter Hennessy’s language is-

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:11.710
Yeah, I know-

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:12.710
Pretty much on the button isn’t it?

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:15.960
I was about to say, if I was sitting where
you're sitting, I'm sure I'd be using the

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:22.300
same colorful language, but it actually conceals
as well as illustrates the nature of democratic

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:23.300
politics.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:27.660
When you have a leader, whether he's a good
one or a bad one, in this case a bad one,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:34.050
when he's been elected by the proper democratic
processes of the Conservative Party and then

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:39.750
given a thumping mandate by the electorate
- you know, the deposition of Margaret Thatcher,

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:46.430
in totally different circumstances, her integrity
wasn't at question in her case, but she was

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:51.780
deposed by her colleagues and not just to
the party, but the country as a whole was

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.300
deeply divided about that.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.270
It did quite a lot- it had to happen.

00:06:55.270 --> 00:06:59.510
I was in the cabinet that helped deliver that
at that time.

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:04.980
So I don't regret what we did, but I'm also
conscious of the fact you create a trauma

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:10.300
which can last and can create other problems
by- if there are still large numbers of people

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:12.720
who believe that an unfair thing has happened.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.430
So you can't rush to such a judgment.

00:07:15.430 --> 00:07:16.970
It can only happen gradually.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:21.050
But do remember: compare Johnson with Trump.

00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:28.930
Trump lost a national election, refused to
go and organized, or was involved in a riot

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:29.930
at the Capitol.

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:36.020
Johnson, with all his terrible faults, which
I'm not personally intending to try and conceal,

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:37.920
at the end of the day, he went quietly.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:38.920
There were no riots.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:42.210
There was no people marching on the House
of Commons to protect him.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:47.800
He simply overnight said, okay, I'm issuing
a statement saying: “I'm stepping down”.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.100
That's how the system ought to work.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:50.100
And it did work.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:55.330
If he leaves office tainted, then the party
is tainted as well.

00:07:55.330 --> 00:08:00.169
Clare Foges, former Downing Street advisor,
wrote in May: “The party - Conservative

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:02.020
Party - is a wrecking ball.

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:05.150
It smashes through parliamentary standards
and public trust.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:10.020
It hurtles through all niceties about the
truth actually mattering.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:13.240
It crashed through our relationship with Europe.”

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:14.240
She has a point.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.400
Johnson still insists that he can leave with
his head held high, he can’t can he?

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:19.400
Of course he can't.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:20.820
Truth is, he leaves in utter disgrace.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:24.090
But the party shares some of that disgrace,
doesn't it?

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:25.090
Well hold on.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:32.099
I mean, again, you're using wonderful, colorful
language and you're using unqualified superlatives.

00:08:32.099 --> 00:08:36.760
What is also true you could have added to
your list of the Conservative Party is that

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:43.190
it has enjoyed the confidence of the British
electorate for 32 of the last 50 years.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:45.330
Yes, but doesn't now, It doesn't now.

00:08:45.330 --> 00:08:46.330
Now.

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.180
Well, at this precise moment, I'm not so sure.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.610
I think it possibly doesn't.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:54.880
But the Labour Party, which is the only credible
alternative government, doesn't inspire that

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:55.920
confidence either.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:58.580
We're going through such a period.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:03.279
Britain is very fortunate actually, is what
we don't have is some ultra-right-wing or

00:09:03.279 --> 00:09:06.480
left-wing party waiting in the eaves to take
over.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.200
We don't have a Marine Le Pen.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:14.140
We don't have either fascist or ultra-socialist
sort of alternatives.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:19.100
So you have a Conservative and a Labour Party
as the two alternative governments.

00:09:19.100 --> 00:09:21.770
At this moment in time Keir Starmer is doing
his best.

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:22.870
Let me try and be objective.

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:28.400
He's a huge improvement on Corbyn, Jeremy
Corbyn, who almost destroyed the Labour Party.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:33.850
But so far as we can tell is the Labour Party
is not at the moment capable of winning an

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:35.930
election by itself and forming a government.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:37.480
So who knows what would happen.

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:39.730
We're in the process of choosing a new prime
minister.

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:40.910
I want to come on to that.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:43.250
That's going to be a major consideration.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:45.100
Yes, I want to come to that.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:51.460
But what should happen to all those who lied
on Johnson's behalf, the enablers who indulged

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:56.150
him, and some who still do, those who defended
him every time a new scandal broke, went to

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:02.430
the TV studios, toured them, giving out the
latest version of events, only to watch it

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:06.403
crumble and disappear a few hours or a few
days later.

00:10:06.403 --> 00:10:08.270
What should happen to these people?

00:10:08.270 --> 00:10:09.870
Because they indulged him, didn't they?

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:10.870
Well, part of-

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:11.870
Allow me to-

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:12.990
-people who kept him in power.

00:10:12.990 --> 00:10:17.241
Allow me to say, there is no government in
the world, democratic or authoritarian, where

00:10:17.241 --> 00:10:23.600
the members of the cabinet accept that the
prime minister or the president, whoever it

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:30.530
may be, it deserves a presumption that he's
telling the truth when he says that some accusation

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:31.530
has been levelled against-

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:33.430
But Johnson has long since trashed that presumption.

00:10:33.430 --> 00:10:34.470
No, no, hold on-

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:35.510
On so many occasions.

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:38.780
Because somebody has lied quite often doesn't
mean everything they say must be a lie.

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:41.750
No, but you can't give him the benefit of
the doubt can you?

00:10:41.750 --> 00:10:43.029
You can't give him the benefit-

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:45.860
Not if he's a habitual liar, can you?

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.370
Well, it depends on the issue.

00:10:47.370 --> 00:10:49.220
It depends on the other evidence that is-

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.220
You're being very kind-

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:51.220
No, I'm not being kind.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.800
I'm trying to be rational and you are for
once being very irrational, as you perfectly

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:56.810
well know.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:11:01.240
What I am saying is that a government, the
government of the country, the Queen's government

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:02.530
has to go on.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:06.690
You don't just sack prime ministers as if
it's a- particularly those who have just a

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:12.230
couple of years ago won a huge majority from
the electorate in a genuine free election.

00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:13.850
It's a serious matter.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:17.420
And the government, whoever is the government-
I was in Margaret Thatcher's government for

00:11:17.420 --> 00:11:22.140
the full period of her term and some of the
time I disagreed strongly with what she was

00:11:22.140 --> 00:11:23.140
doing.

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:28.050
If you feel strongly on a particular matter
for which you have the departmental responsibility,

00:11:28.050 --> 00:11:29.810
then you resign.

00:11:29.810 --> 00:11:34.360
But all the time when the Cabinet reaches
a decision, that decision will be presented

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:38.920
as a unanimous decision of the Cabinet because
those who are in the minority are willing

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:39.920
to go along with it.

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:40.960
So you're telling me that

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:46.000
That’s what happens in any democratic country
at any time and it couldn't happen otherwise.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:48.130
Otherwise governments would be collapsing
every six months.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:51.740
But you're telling me now that there's no
need for a clean out of all the old officials?

00:11:51.740 --> 00:11:52.740
-I never said that.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:55.330
Is there a need for a clean out of the party
officials?

00:11:55.330 --> 00:11:56.330
Johnson's acolytes?

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.660
In order to restore public trust?

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.470
You're using nice, neutral phrases like Johnson's
acolytes.

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:08.990
There are some members of his team who are
not my flavor of the month, as it were, who

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:13.580
I hope- whose services I hope will not be
required by the new prime minister.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.430
And I'm not going to name names in this particular
interview because that's not my function.

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:17.680
I'm a private citizen.

00:12:17.680 --> 00:12:19.860
I don't see the need to.

00:12:19.860 --> 00:12:25.890
But of course I hope the new prime minister,
whoever he or she might be, will use the opportunity.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:30.640
And actually, the most important thing I think
the new prime minister should do is create

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:34.649
a cabinet of all the talents, because Johnson
didn't do that.

00:12:34.649 --> 00:12:39.310
Johnson surrounded himself with some people
he'd surrounded himself with were of cabinet

00:12:39.310 --> 00:12:40.310
stature.

00:12:40.310 --> 00:12:43.530
Some, frankly, should never have been in the
Cabinet in the first place.

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:48.470
Sir Malcolm, there's a lot of talk about restoring
trust and integrity in government, but already

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:53.690
some in your party are writing almost Pravda
style or the alternative history of the Johnson

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:54.690
era.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:57.770
The pretense is that he got all the big calls
right.

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:00.220
But he patently didn't, did he?

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:05.490
Firstly, you're asking me to say what I've
already said in public many, many times.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:08.620
Of course, he got a lot of decisions, in my
view, badly wrong.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:09.620
Some were right.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:15.320
Actually, to be fair to him on Ukraine, he
has provided a degree of leadership, of a

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:18.480
very impressive kind that took me by surprise.

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:24.020
And he's been, apart from the United States,
the leading champion of Ukraine and perceived

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:25.990
by the Ukrainians themselves as one of their
closest friends.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.520
But that version is slightly misleading, isn't
it?

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:33.700
The truth is that for seven years following
Russia's invasion of Crimea, Britain refused

00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:38.490
to send weapons that Kyiv needed, despite
the fact that they were asking for them on

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:39.500
a regular basis.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:41.400
Why isn't that acknowledged?

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.089
Sorry, if you wish to move on from what you
were previously asking, please acknowledge

00:13:45.089 --> 00:13:46.399
that's what you're doing.

00:13:46.399 --> 00:13:48.760
You can't blame Johnson for what happened
in the last seven years.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:50.680
Well, for the time that he was foreign secretary.

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:52.950
He wasn't prime minister, and he didn't control
the government.

00:13:52.950 --> 00:13:55.230
And I don't know what internal discussions
took place then.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:59.250
I know, for example, because it's become public
that Ben Wallace, as defense secretary, was

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:04.160
arguing at an earlier time for more practical
help, military help, to be given to the Ukrainians.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.960
But that wasn't happening, either in Britain
or the United States or in other Western countries.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:09.830
This is a British phenomenon.

00:14:09.830 --> 00:14:15.730
What is true, and it should be acknowledged,
is that before the invasion of Crimea, the

00:14:15.730 --> 00:14:21.279
only two Western countries that were giving
serious military help to Ukraine were the

00:14:21.279 --> 00:14:26.560
United States and the United Kingdom, and
they were doing so by, for example, military

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:30.650
training — openly, it wasn't secret — some
of it might have been secret, some of it was

00:14:30.650 --> 00:14:31.650
quite open.

00:14:31.650 --> 00:14:33.720
The British military personnel were training.

00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:36.800
How do you think Ukraine was able in six years?

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:42.330
Six years ago, at the time of Crimea's annexation,
Ukraine's army was about 10,000 strong.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:45.730
When Putin invaded, it was 120,000 strong.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:51.070
So they not only had to build up an army,
but it was trained in different concepts of

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:56.089
warfare to what the old Soviet Union used
to do and what Russia still does, which is

00:14:56.089 --> 00:15:00.839
why in the first few weeks of the invasion
of Ukraine, the Ukrainians were able to hold

00:15:00.839 --> 00:15:03.060
on to Kyiv and Putin was humiliated.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:10.300
Now that was because British and American
military personnel had been actively involved

00:15:10.300 --> 00:15:11.600
in giving advice.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:17.130
You could only give advice, not instructions,
to the Ukrainian military before the invasion

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:18.372
as to how they should operate.

00:15:18.372 --> 00:15:21.570
And the second point, if I may, is on cyber.

00:15:21.570 --> 00:15:24.760
We all assumed that if there was going to
be a Russian attack on Ukraine, the first

00:15:24.760 --> 00:15:29.910
thing they would do in 24 hours would have
been to completely neutralize the whole infrastructure

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:32.310
in Ukraine through hacking and cyberattacks.

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:33.840
They weren't able to do that.

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:34.840
Why?

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:41.540
Because we now know that GCHQ, Britain's special
intelligence agency that deals with these

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:47.000
matters, was authorized by the British government
to give lots of advice to the Ukrainians over

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:52.820
the last few years, as was happening from
others as well, on how to deal with cyberattacks

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.000
and protect your infrastructure.

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:57.300
You talk about what was done before Putin
actually decided to invade.

00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:58.410
To invade, yes.

00:15:58.410 --> 00:16:02.490
But it wasn't until June last year that the
government agreed to help Ukraine rebuild

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:04.769
its Navy, but by then it was too late.

00:16:04.769 --> 00:16:06.560
The ships weren't ready for the invasion.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:10.270
Ukraine's ambassador, Vadym Prystaiko, said:
"We had to scrap the project.

00:16:10.270 --> 00:16:12.920
It was just delay after delay."

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:14.680
Doesn't that sadden you?

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:19.530
Well, in my own personal case, I would have
liked more practical help to be given earlier.

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:23.310
That was my public view, and so I'm not going
to pretend otherwise.

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:28.890
But, you know, it's a pretty controversial
question, because Ukraine is not a member

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:30.920
of NATO.

00:16:30.920 --> 00:16:35.610
And therefore, the provision of military assistance
to a country that might be involved in a war

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:40.521
in the short- to medium-term is a very complex
question, not just for the United Kingdom.

00:16:40.521 --> 00:16:44.630
But they were involved in a war in the east
of the country, a war fueled by Russia, weren't

00:16:44.630 --> 00:16:45.630
they.

00:16:45.630 --> 00:16:46.630
Yes, of course, of course.

00:16:46.630 --> 00:16:47.630
They have been fighting since 2014.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:48.630
Yes.

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:49.640
But that had become a frozen conflict.

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:54.210
And I was talking about a new war of the kind
that we now have experienced.

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:59.720
So the United States and the United Kingdom
might have responded to Ukraine's needs slower

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:04.010
than the Ukrainians would have liked, but
they responded a damn sight quicker than France

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:07.509
or Germany or any other NATO country or any
other country in the world.

00:17:07.509 --> 00:17:09.269
And the Ukrainians have acknowledged that.

00:17:09.269 --> 00:17:14.209
Let's talk, if we may, about Boris Johnson's
unusual relations — let's put it that way

00:17:14.209 --> 00:17:16.879
— with with Russia and Russians.

00:17:16.879 --> 00:17:22.199
In 2018, a month after the Russians used Novichok
to try and kill Sergei Skripal and his daughter

00:17:22.199 --> 00:17:27.990
in Salisbury, Johnson flew to Italy, minus
his security detail and his officials, and

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:33.489
attended a party at which he met a former
KGB oligarch, Alexander Lebedev.

00:17:33.489 --> 00:17:39.120
No details of those discussions were ever
made public, and it took four years for the

00:17:39.120 --> 00:17:43.240
prime minister to actually confirm that the
meeting had taken place.

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:46.759
Is that lack of accountability acceptable
for a British foreign secretary?

00:17:46.759 --> 00:17:47.759
No, no.

00:17:47.759 --> 00:17:48.759
It's not.

00:17:48.759 --> 00:17:50.020
What do you read into that, then?

00:17:50.020 --> 00:17:52.399
Well, nothing that I didn't already know.

00:17:52.399 --> 00:17:58.489
That Johnson has the long history of being
reckless, of doing things without accepting

00:17:58.489 --> 00:17:59.989
the implications of them.

00:17:59.989 --> 00:18:03.580
And, of course, as foreign secretary, if he
was going abroad and meeting people of that

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:07.250
kind, the Foreign Office should have been
aware of that — for his own protection,

00:18:07.250 --> 00:18:08.480
apart from any other reasons.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:13.380
So these are all reasons why quite a number
of us never thought he was suitable to be

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:15.009
prime minister in the first place.

00:18:15.009 --> 00:18:20.359
Sadly, that was not the view of the majority
of parliamentarians or of the public, who

00:18:20.359 --> 00:18:22.799
gave him power at the last general election.

00:18:22.799 --> 00:18:24.769
Was he soft on Russia, in your opinion?

00:18:24.769 --> 00:18:30.480
Two parliamentary inquiries in 2018 and 2020,
when he was prime minister, called for sanctions

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:32.700
to be imposed on Russian oligarchs.

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:34.179
They were largely ignored.

00:18:34.179 --> 00:18:40.539
I wasn't involved in government, so I don't
know the details of what went on at that time.

00:18:40.539 --> 00:18:44.980
So I'm not going to offer a judgment on why
that was not done, because I simply don't

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:45.980
know.

00:18:45.980 --> 00:18:49.659
Is one of the reasons that he may have gone
soft on Russia, the fact that so much Russian

00:18:49.659 --> 00:18:53.830
money was pouring into the Tory party coffers
at the time?

00:18:53.830 --> 00:18:55.350
No, insofar as the...

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:57.139
They did get a lot of Russian money.

00:18:57.139 --> 00:19:01.759
Well, you talk of Russian money as if it's
coming from the Kremlin, and you know perfectly

00:19:01.759 --> 00:19:02.759
well it isn't.

00:19:02.759 --> 00:19:05.179
We know perfectly well it's very difficult
to say...

00:19:05.179 --> 00:19:06.179
Now hold on.

00:19:06.179 --> 00:19:07.179
...where it's coming from.

00:19:07.179 --> 00:19:08.179
No, no.

00:19:08.179 --> 00:19:09.600
Look, first of all, I'm not going to go into
the detail of this, partly because I don't

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:14.090
know the detail, and partly because I think
you're on pretty tricky ground here.

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:18.399
First of all, the law is you can only accept
political donations from British citizens.

00:19:18.399 --> 00:19:23.570
Now, there happen to be a number of people,
not just Russians, but of other nationalities,

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:27.419
who've acquired British citizenship, who are
living law-abiding, so far as we know, lives

00:19:27.419 --> 00:19:31.009
in the United Kingdom and some of whom have
donated to the Conservative Party.

00:19:31.009 --> 00:19:35.499
You cannot describe that as Russian money,
which to your viewers will sound as if it's

00:19:35.499 --> 00:19:40.830
Putin bribing British people, a British political
party.

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:44.080
That's complete rubbish and you know perfectly
well it is.

00:19:44.080 --> 00:19:49.369
You were chair of the Intelligence and Security
Committee 2010 to 2015, which gets to ask

00:19:49.369 --> 00:19:51.420
questions that the public don't get to ask.

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:52.420
And to get answers.

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:55.480
And to get answers that the public doesn't
hear, either.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:20:00.109
Johnson refused multiple efforts by MPs to
investigate whether Russia had interfered

00:20:00.109 --> 00:20:02.749
with the Brexit referendum.

00:20:02.749 --> 00:20:08.729
The Government said: "a retrospective assessment
of the EU referendum is not necessary."

00:20:08.729 --> 00:20:12.369
How could Johnson have known that when he
didn't even ask the security services to find

00:20:12.369 --> 00:20:13.369
out?

00:20:13.369 --> 00:20:14.369
Sure.

00:20:14.369 --> 00:20:15.779
Well, I'd make an additional point to what
you just said.

00:20:15.779 --> 00:20:21.179
The Intelligence and Security Committee can,
is free to investigate any subject it wishes.

00:20:21.179 --> 00:20:24.919
It does not need the prime minister's permission
to do so.

00:20:24.919 --> 00:20:27.989
It needs his permission to publish its report.

00:20:27.989 --> 00:20:30.230
Which was long delayed by Johnson.

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:31.729
Yes, that's a separate point.

00:20:31.729 --> 00:20:39.799
But the actual report itself and the investigation,
if it was felt by the all-party Intelligence

00:20:39.799 --> 00:20:43.840
and Security Committee — I chaired it for
five years and we gave it additional new powers.

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:47.970
Parliament gave it the sort of powers it needed
so that it was not dependent on the prime

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:51.739
minister of the day as to whether it could
make investigations of this kind.

00:20:51.739 --> 00:20:55.840
So the presumption of your question is just
not valid.

00:20:55.840 --> 00:20:59.460
Let's look at the contest to replace Boris
Johnson.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:00.950
Three candidates left as we speak.

00:21:00.950 --> 00:21:03.460
I know that later on today they're going to
be just two.

00:21:03.460 --> 00:21:06.600
All of them were Johnson appointees.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:09.979
Whoever wins, will it just be more of the
same?

00:21:09.979 --> 00:21:12.229
Well, I hope not.

00:21:12.229 --> 00:21:17.059
And I have no reason to believe it because
they are very different per-, I do mean, they're

00:21:17.059 --> 00:21:18.179
very different from each other.

00:21:18.179 --> 00:21:20.679
All three of them are very different from
Johnson.

00:21:20.679 --> 00:21:22.840
Sunak, Mordaunt, Truss.

00:21:22.840 --> 00:21:30.759
There has no, that I'm aware of, been any
type of public criticism remotely of a kind

00:21:30.759 --> 00:21:34.450
that correctly addressed itself to Boris Johnson.

00:21:34.450 --> 00:21:39.350
And over the last couple of weeks, if there
was anything new to discovered about them,

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:44.289
then that, I suspect, would have been on the
headlines of every newspaper in Britain.

00:21:44.289 --> 00:21:46.359
But there — nothing there was.

00:21:46.359 --> 00:21:53.440
We must assume that they have — all three
of them have — a history of public service,

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.620
of integrity.

00:21:54.620 --> 00:21:57.889
And I'm more in favor of some than others.

00:21:57.889 --> 00:22:00.679
But that's, that's a question of personal
preference.

00:22:00.679 --> 00:22:04.669
But their integrity has not been a major issue
that I'm aware of.

00:22:04.669 --> 00:22:09.950
The two debates so far have revealed very
little, apart from huge animosity between

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:14.639
the various candidates, so much so that the
contestants pulled out of the third debate,

00:22:14.639 --> 00:22:18.769
although there will be one between the final
two.

00:22:18.769 --> 00:22:21.970
This is a party very much at war with itself,
isn't it?

00:22:21.970 --> 00:22:23.960
It's a divided party, isn't it.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:24.960
No.

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:25.960
Come on, come on.

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:26.960
Seriously divided.

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:27.960
No, no.

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:33.090
Whenever you have, in any democratic party,
an open competition to choose a new leader

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:36.480
who, if you're in government, will become
the prime minister or president, depending

00:22:36.480 --> 00:22:40.269
on which country we're talking about, of course,
that's democracy, actually.

00:22:40.269 --> 00:22:44.659
You know, if they pretended they agreed with
each other all the time, you'd be saying to

00:22:44.659 --> 00:22:48.419
me, ‘they seem a bunch of clones, there's
nothing — you can't tell one from the other,

00:22:48.419 --> 00:22:50.720
all saying exactly the same thing.’

00:22:50.720 --> 00:22:51.789
That would be your criticism.

00:22:51.789 --> 00:22:55.669
Instead, now what we are seeing is a democracy
at work.

00:22:55.669 --> 00:23:00.710
And of course within a major political party,
like — not just the Conservative Party,

00:23:00.710 --> 00:23:04.960
the same applies to the Labour Party or any
other major party — you have a broad church.

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:09.210
You know, I was not a Thatcherite when I was
in Mrs. Thatcher's cabinet.

00:23:09.210 --> 00:23:12.619
There were a number of things I disagreed
with — that's normal.

00:23:12.619 --> 00:23:18.259
And you try not to wash your dirty linen in
public, as it were, but if you're actually

00:23:18.259 --> 00:23:22.769
standing for office, the public are entitled
to know what are, not just things you share

00:23:22.769 --> 00:23:25.470
in common with your colleagues, but what are
the differences?

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:28.929
You'd include the European Research Group
in the, in the "broad church" description.

00:23:28.929 --> 00:23:31.440
Of course they are.

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:32.669
They are all Conservatives.

00:23:32.669 --> 00:23:33.669
They take the Conservative way.

00:23:33.669 --> 00:23:37.869
And their Brexit orthodoxy, that nobody is
allowed to challenge without being set on?

00:23:37.869 --> 00:23:41.429
You say "nobody is allowed to challenge without
being set on" — they're as entitled as you

00:23:41.429 --> 00:23:43.970
or I am to criticize people they disagree
with.

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:45.519
Well, they get shouted down, don't they?

00:23:45.519 --> 00:23:47.529
The big lie is that Brexit is marvelous.

00:23:47.529 --> 00:23:48.529
No, hold on.

00:23:48.529 --> 00:23:49.529
Isn't it?

00:23:49.529 --> 00:23:54.029
I am not a supporter of the European Research
Group because — the people you're referring

00:23:54.029 --> 00:23:55.029
to.

00:23:55.029 --> 00:24:00.019
But they have exactly the same political right
as any other political animal, any other member

00:24:00.019 --> 00:24:04.049
of Parliament, any other member of the public
— dare I say it, even any other journalist

00:24:04.049 --> 00:24:10.149
— to condemn views they disapprove of and
to criticize people who are arguing for things

00:24:10.149 --> 00:24:12.000
they think would be against the national interest.

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.740
What's wrong with that, for crying out loud?

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.139
That's what democracy is about.

00:24:15.139 --> 00:24:17.549
Has nobody ever told you that.

00:24:17.549 --> 00:24:19.450
What's the future for this party?

00:24:19.450 --> 00:24:20.450
Democratic?

00:24:20.450 --> 00:24:22.239
Democratic, or divided into wings?

00:24:22.239 --> 00:24:29.220
You know, hard right, pushing Brexit and getting
rid of all the criticism out there?

00:24:29.220 --> 00:24:33.299
Can you think of one thing about Brexit that
has worked well?

00:24:33.299 --> 00:24:34.630
Well, I was a remainer, so...

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:35.630
I know.

00:24:35.630 --> 00:24:38.570
But I'm asking you, can you think of one thing
about Brexit that has worked well?

00:24:38.570 --> 00:24:39.570
What?

00:24:39.570 --> 00:24:40.570
Yes.

00:24:40.570 --> 00:24:41.570
Well, the country's calmed down.

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:42.570
Really?

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:43.570
Yes.

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:44.570
I'll tell you what exactly...

00:24:44.570 --> 00:24:45.570
The country isn't working.

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:47.039
I'm talking about, we're talking about Brexit,
I think.

00:24:47.039 --> 00:24:48.039
Yes.

00:24:48.039 --> 00:24:49.039
Yes.

00:24:49.039 --> 00:24:50.039
Right.

00:24:50.039 --> 00:24:51.889
Now, what I'm saying to you, I don't think
you would challenge, certainly not privately.

00:24:51.889 --> 00:24:56.509
You might choose to for the purpose of this
interview, is that for a number of years,

00:24:56.509 --> 00:25:02.289
for about ten years, Britain was deeply divided,
including the referendum itself, on whether

00:25:02.289 --> 00:25:07.700
— and families were divided and communities
were divided, and there was a lot of aggro

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:08.700
about that.

00:25:08.700 --> 00:25:09.700
It's still divided.

00:25:09.700 --> 00:25:10.700
Let me finish my answer.

00:25:10.700 --> 00:25:16.119
And it was dominating the British political
scene at the expense of almost anything else.

00:25:16.119 --> 00:25:19.299
Now, if it could be resolved, it had to be
resolved.

00:25:19.299 --> 00:25:23.460
And if it was resolved by a large majority
or whatever majority it was, saying we were

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:26.789
going to stay in, then hopefully it would
have calmed down as well.

00:25:26.789 --> 00:25:29.809
But the important thing is that the issue
has gone off the boil.

00:25:29.809 --> 00:25:32.700
Mr. Farage, God bless him, has disappeared.

00:25:32.700 --> 00:25:37.429
Nobody is remotely interested in him or his
party because the issue itself is not dominating

00:25:37.429 --> 00:25:38.429
British politics.

00:25:38.429 --> 00:25:41.200
Sir Malcolm Rifkind, we have to disappear,
unfortunately.

00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:42.200
Thanks.

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:43.200
Thanks very much indeed for being on.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:44.200
Thank you very much.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:44.202
Thank you.

